= Claim validation =

'''Claim validation is the examination of whether a particular assertion is adequately supported, within which boundaries, and with what remaining uncertainty.''' It is a process of critical assessment, not a declaration of absolute truth or a mechanical test based on source count, credentials or confidence labels.

An assertion may be well established in one context and unsupported in another. The relevant questions are: '''What exactly is claimed? What would demonstrate it? What does the available material establish? What would change our assessment?'''

This article explains the process. [[BITwiki:Evidence]] describes evidential support, [[BITwiki:Sources]] examines source credibility and [[BITwiki:Provenance]] traces how a claim reached its present form.

== 1. Define the assertion ==

Identify the proposition precisely enough that an assessment can succeed or fail meaningfully.

* '''Meaning:''' Which terms require definitions or refer to contested constructs?
* '''Kind:''' Is the claim empirical, causal, mathematical, historical, technical, normative, experiential or interpretive?
* '''Scope:''' What population, system, location, version, period or boundary conditions does it cover?
* '''Strength:''' Does the wording say “may,” “is associated with,” “causes,” “always,” or “has been proved”? These express different degrees of commitment.
* '''Discriminating evidence:''' What observation, counterexample, proof, record or argument would distinguish this claim from alternatives?

A broad slogan should be replaced or qualified by a proposition that can actually be examined. Where a statement is philosophical or normative rather than empirical, assess its premises and arguments without pretending that an experiment settles the entire question.

== 2. Match the method to the question ==

{| class="wikitable"
! Claim type !! Appropriate checks !! Common error
|-
| Empirical description || Sampling, instrument validity, observational conditions, uncertainty estimates, independent observation || Generalizing beyond the measured setting
|-
| Causal claim || Comparisons, causal identification, controls, confounders, temporal ordering and alternative mechanisms || Treating association as proof of causality
|-
| Mathematical or formal result || Premises, definitions, derivation, proof and counterexample || Confusing an internally valid model with a true description of nature
|-
| Historical claim || Original records, authentication, chronology, contextual interpretation and corroboration || Treating a contemporaneous assertion as an independently verified event
|-
| Technical or software claim || Specification, exact version, source inspection, test procedure, reproducible behavior and deployment conditions || Inferring production behavior from a proposed design
|-
| Philosophical or interpretive claim || Clarity, argumentative validity, textual fidelity, objections and alternative interpretations || Requiring empirical falsification of a non-empirical proposition
|-
| First-person or practitioner claim || Accurate attribution, circumstances, triangulation where possible and explicit limits || Elevating one experience into a universal pattern
|}

Falsifiability is important for many empirical hypotheses but is not a universal criterion for all meaningful inquiry.

== 3. Verify the evidence and its provenance ==

# Locate the original material where practical.
# Confirm that the cited work exists, identify its version and read the relevant passage or data.
# Separate what the source directly establishes from what an editor, author or machine inferred.
# Examine whether additional citations supply independent evidence or repeat the same ancestor.
# Identify relevant conflicts, corrections, funding, publication history and missing records.
# Record the chain of transformation when paraphrase, translation, summarization or automated synthesis materially affects meaning.

A citation can be genuine and still fail to support the attached sentence. In high-impact or contested contexts, follow the support chain rather than relying on an abstract, headline or secondary quotation.

== 4. Assess methods, uncertainty and alternative explanations ==

Question-specific considerations include:

* adequacy of measurements, definitions, sampling and controls;
* analytical validity and material modeling assumptions;
* effect sizes, measurement error and uncertainty intervals, where meaningful;
* missing data, selection bias, confounding and selective publication;
* reproducibility of computation and replication through independent observations, where applicable;
* applicability of results beyond the original conditions;
* contradictory evidence, competing interpretations and known limitations.

For intervention research, the [https://www.cochrane.org/authors/handbooks-and-manuals/handbook/current Cochrane Handbook] and [https://www.bmj.com/content/389/bmj-2024-081903 GRADE approach] provide examples of evaluating risk of bias, inconsistency, indirectness, imprecision and publication bias. Those tools have defined purposes; they are not universal templates for history, mathematics, philosophy or engineering.

The absence of published corroboration is not always evidence that a phenomenon is absent. Consider whether the relevant question has been investigated by a method capable of detecting it.

== 5. Form a calibrated conclusion ==

Separate three judgments:

* '''Evidentiary support:''' What follows from the observations, proofs, records or arguments?
* '''Uncertainty:''' Which limitations or competing explanations remain, and why do they matter?
* '''Scope of reliance:''' Under what conditions is the conclusion reasonable to use or cite?

A confidence explanation is more informative than a label alone. For example, an assessment might state:

<blockquote>
Several independent studies report an effect under the tested conditions, but estimates vary and evidence outside the studied population remains limited. A larger independent replication could materially change the estimated effect.
</blockquote>

A qualitative [[BITwiki:Epistemic status|epistemic standing]] can accompany that reasoning when useful. A numerical probability requires a justified and interpretable model or calibration method; do not assign numbers simply to create an appearance of precision. Terms such as “verified” should say '''what''' was checked, '''how''', and under which conditions.

== 6. Make the validation traceable ==

A concise review record or article discussion should allow another reader to reconstruct the judgment:

{| class="wikitable"
! Element !! Record
|-
| Claim || Exact wording and its scope
|-
| Evidence || Sources, specific passages, measurements, tests or arguments
|-
| Method || How support and alternatives were assessed
|-
| Limitations || Contradictions, uncertainties, dependencies and boundaries
|-
| Judgment || Supported conclusion with reasons, not an unexplained score
|-
| Revision conditions || What evidence or event would prompt reassessment
|}

Not every paragraph requires a form or dedicated semantic property. The appropriate record depends on importance, complexity and risk of error. A completed checklist is not itself validation.

== Worked example: a software performance claim ==

Consider the '''illustrative''' assertion: “Release R reduces median API response time by 20%.” No actual product or measured improvement is being claimed here.

A valid assessment would identify the baseline release, measurement window, test hardware, workload, response-time definition, number of runs, variation and method of comparison. It would check whether the 20% reduction was computed correctly and whether caches, traffic patterns or other changes could explain it. A source-code change or release note alone does not establish that production performance improved.

The supported conclusion might be limited to: “Under benchmark configuration B, release R showed a 20% lower measured median response time than release Q.” It would not justify “all users experience a 20% speed increase” without additional evidence. The test record, versions and limitations should remain inspectable.

== Review, disagreement and correction ==

Re-examine a claim when new material changes its support, a source is corrected or retracted, replication fails, methods change, a disputed interpretation becomes clearer, or the claim is reused in a higher-stakes setting.

Disagreement can identify a real contradiction, different definitions, or distinct boundary conditions. Represent the strongest relevant alternatives fairly; do not suppress contrary results or suggest that all views are equally well supported.

Recurring failures worth detecting include source misattribution, citation laundering, selective reporting, unsupported causal inference, exaggerated certainty, invented references, false precision and the appearance of validation produced by unexamined checklists.

In BITwiki, publication indicates editorial responsibility for an account; it does '''not''' establish that an accepted claim is infallible. Revisions should preserve enough source and reasoning context for readers to see what changed and why.

